From a1447154331c061d04584b7381b7062e10926131 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dfattal <dfattal@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 22:10:01 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] inline-3d(web#12): resolve before suppressing, and never
 weave the hole
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The dark tile that survived 0070, 0071 and 0072 is a set asymmetry in Viz, and
the frames it ruins report success everywhere.

Display::DrawAndSwap builds two lists from the same aggregated frame. The weave
input is the post-resolve mailbox list, which silently drops any canvas layer
whose ResourceId has no SharedImage mailbox. The suppression set is keyed on
bare ResourceId and drops nothing. So a layer that failed to resolve was pulled
out of the page raster and never handed to the weave. The GPU stage then found
no clean canvas resource for that tile and fell back to snapshotting the
composited output at the tile rect — which, since browser#22 landed suppression,
is exactly the hole that was just punched. It wove the hole, the batch submitted,
attempt_submit returned true, and 0070's recovery is gated on !wove_this_frame so
it was unreachable by construction. Zero submit failures, zero AcquireSync
timeouts, zero view flips, and a black tile. The shared weave input is never
cleared between frames, which is why consecutive captures of a blink burst came
out byte-identical and read as a stale misassembly.

Four parts.

Root: resolve first, suppress only what survived (display.cc). Never punch a hole
you cannot fill. A tile whose resource is gone now stays an ordinary page quad
for that frame instead of becoming a woven hole.

Defence: attempt_submit refuses to weave |output| wherever it may be holed. The
suppression set and inline_3d_canvas_layers_ are now the same list, so that list
answers the question on its own — empty means nothing was suppressed and the
composited snapshot is still the legitimate legacy weave source; non-empty means
canvas quads were removed and a tile with no clean resource must be skipped, not
snapshotted. The same gate covers the CPU-readback fallback, which sources ONLY
from |output| and had no gate at all, and the case where a resolved resource
fails to open.

Recovery: 0070's mono repaint becomes per-tile. It closed the loop at the frame
level and left it open at the tile level — a frame where tile A wove and tile B
did not left B black. The two callers share one lambda; the per-tile form runs
before the phase (B) draw-back, which is safe because the runtime clears its
woven output to transparent each frame, so kSrcOver leaves the repaint intact.
Part 1 and this cannot double-draw: an unsuppressed layer never reaches the GPU
stage's list, so the loop cannot see it.

Counters, on 0070's discipline (LOG(ERROR), first 3 then 1-in-300), because every
step of this mechanism was silent: resolve drops, no-clean-resource skips,
CPU-readback hole skips, zero-copy batches (the discriminator for the multi-second
no-submit windows), the canvas-layer lookup returning nothing (logged NOWHERE
before), and the id-miss and recovery-draw lines promoted off VLOG(1), where
"no lines" was never evidence of absence. The root-render-pass size bail counts
consecutive misses and speaks only after a run, since it fires harmlessly on
every element-sized pass.

Also fixes the latent keyed-mutex leak next door: the mixed-format guard asked
about batch_entries_ when the thing at risk is the mutex, so a target that
acquired the input and was then rejected by the destination clamp could let a
differently-formatted target recreate the shared texture underneath the held
mutex.

Validated per-TU with clang-cl -fsyntax-only (all three TUs clean, tripwire
confirms the check bites).

Refs: DisplayXR/displayxr-web#12
Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc      |  35 +-
 components/viz/service/display/display.cc     |  92 +++--
 .../skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc        | 336 ++++++++++++++----
 3 files changed, 378 insertions(+), 85 deletions(-)

diff --git a/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc b/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc
index a5665b2f0e329..d239344dd6c1a 100644
--- a/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc
+++ b/components/displayxr/gpu/displayxr_weave_gpu.cc
@@ -504,8 +504,20 @@ bool DisplayXRWeaveGpu::EnsureBatchInputAndAcquire(ID3D11Device* device,
   // All of a frame's copies share ONE input; a mid-frame format mismatch would
   // recreate it under the held mutex and lose the earlier copies. In practice
   // every canvas copy comes from the same composited output format.
-  if (!batch_entries_.empty() && shared_input_.input_tex &&
-      shared_input_.input_format != format) {
+  //
+  // (web#12) The guard used to test |batch_entries_| alone, which is the wrong
+  // question: a target can ACQUIRE the input and then be rejected by the
+  // destination clamp in BatchCopyCanvas without ever pushing an entry. The
+  // batch is then mutex-held with an empty entry list, this guard waves a
+  // differently-formatted target through, EnsureInput destroys and recreates
+  // the shared texture underneath the held mutex, and the old mutex is never
+  // released while the new one is never acquired — |batch_mutex_held_| now
+  // describes a texture that no longer exists. Latent (it needs a clamp
+  // rejection and a format change in the same frame), but the failure is a
+  // stuck keyed mutex, which is exactly the class this whole issue family lives
+  // in. Ask about the mutex, since the mutex is what is at risk.
+  if ((batch_mutex_held_ || !batch_entries_.empty()) &&
+      shared_input_.input_tex && shared_input_.input_format != format) {
     static bool logged_fmt = false;
     if (!logged_fmt) {
       logged_fmt = true;
@@ -650,6 +662,7 @@ bool DisplayXRWeaveGpu::SubmitBatchSync() {
     return false;
   }
   batch_open_ = false;
+  const bool had_mutex = batch_mutex_held_;
   const bool had_copies = batch_mutex_held_ && !batch_entries_.empty();
   if (batch_mutex_held_) {
     // Hand the input to the service in the "caller done writing" state.
@@ -658,6 +671,24 @@ bool DisplayXRWeaveGpu::SubmitBatchSync() {
     batch_mutex_held_ = false;
   }
   if (!had_copies) {
+    // (web#12) A batch that copied NOTHING issues no submit at all, so the
+    // runtime never re-weaves, never runs its first-chunk transparent clear,
+    // and never even polls its capture trigger — which is what turned a stall
+    // into a multi-second window that read, from the outside, as "the weave is
+    // frozen" with not one line in either log. It was completely silent. It is
+    // now the discriminator for that window: when every target declines (a
+    // canvas resource that will not open, a readback that returns nothing, or —
+    // since this patch — every tile refusing to weave the page's suppression
+    // hole) this is the line that says so. First 3, then 1-in-300, matching the
+    // submit-failure counter below.
+    static int s_empty = 0;
+    const int n = s_empty++;
+    if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+      LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] batch weave: ZERO copies this frame — no "
+                    "submit issued (acquired_input="
+                 << had_mutex << " entries=" << batch_entries_.size()
+                 << " empties=" << (n + 1) << ")";
+    }
     return false;
   }
 
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display/display.cc b/components/viz/service/display/display.cc
index 125abbb6553e5..d652a9f8cd398 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display/display.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display/display.cc
@@ -986,26 +986,73 @@ bool Display::DrawAndSwap(const DrawAndSwapParams& params) {
     // canvas rects to the GPU thread before DrawFrame (which enqueues
     // FinishPaintCurrentFrame), so the post-paint output weave loop reads the
     // rects that match this frame's composited pixels.
-    if (skia_output_surface_) {
+    //
+    // ---- (web#12) RESOLVE BEFORE SUPPRESSING — one set, not two ----
+    //
+    // Two lists are built here from the same aggregated frame, and until this
+    // patch they were NOT the same set:
+    //   * the WEAVE INPUT — the post-resolve mailbox list handed to the GPU
+    //     stage, which SILENTLY DROPPED any layer whose ResourceId did not
+    //     resolve to a SharedImage mailbox;
+    //   * the SUPPRESSION set — keyed on bare ResourceId, which dropped
+    //     nothing.
+    // A layer that failed to resolve was therefore removed from the page raster
+    // (direct_renderer.cc drops the quad) while never reaching the weave. The
+    // GPU stage then found no clean canvas resource for that tile, fell back to
+    // snapshotting the composited output at the tile rect — which is EXACTLY
+    // the hole suppression had just punched — wove it, and reported success. A
+    // dark tile on a frame with zero errors anywhere: the fourth and root
+    // dark-tile path (after #99's missed submit, #92's stuck degraded mode and
+    // this issue's rig flips).
+    //
+    // So resolve FIRST and suppress only what survived. NEVER PUNCH A HOLE YOU
+    // CANNOT FILL. A tile whose resource is gone then simply stays an ordinary
+    // page quad for that frame — the page draws it exactly as it would with no
+    // inline-3D at all — instead of becoming a woven hole. That is a strictly
+    // better failure: the weave skips the tile, and the tile is still there.
+    //
+    // Ordering is safe by construction: both sets are built here, in
+    // DrawAndSwap, BEFORE renderer_->DrawFrame() below consumes the suppression
+    // set, and the resolve is a pure read of the resource provider Display
+    // already owns.
+    std::vector<SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dLayerMailbox> resolved_canvas;
+    ResourceIdSet resolved_canvas_ids;
+    {
       // Resolve each isolated canvas layer's ResourceId → its SharedImage
       // mailbox here (Display owns the resource provider), handing the GPU
       // stage plain {mailbox, rect} — the same shape the render-pass path used.
       // browser#18.
-      auto resolve =
-          [this](
-              const std::vector<AggregatedFrame::Inline3dLayerResource>& in) {
-            std::vector<SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dLayerMailbox> out;
-            out.reserve(in.size());
-            for (const auto& layer : in) {
-              gpu::Mailbox m =
-                  resource_provider_->GetMailbox(layer.resource_id);
-              if (!m.IsZero()) {
-                out.push_back(
-                    {m, layer.rect, layer.visible_rect, layer.uv, layer.id});
-              }
-            }
-            return out;
-          };
+      int dropped = 0;
+      resolved_canvas.reserve(frame.inline_3d_canvas_resources.size());
+      for (const auto& layer : frame.inline_3d_canvas_resources) {
+        gpu::Mailbox m = resource_provider_->GetMailbox(layer.resource_id);
+        if (m.IsZero()) {
+          ++dropped;
+          continue;
+        }
+        resolved_canvas.push_back(
+            {m, layer.rect, layer.visible_rect, layer.uv, layer.id});
+        resolved_canvas_ids.insert(layer.resource_id);
+      }
+      // (web#12) This drop was invisible for the whole life of the bug — it is
+      // the event that used to desynchronize the two sets, so it must be
+      // countable now that it no longer does. LOG(ERROR), first 3 (a startup
+      // burst is never throttled away) then 1-in-300 (~5 s at 60 fps): a page
+      // that starts dropping ten minutes in must still leave a trace.
+      if (dropped > 0) {
+        static int s_drop = 0;
+        const int n = s_drop++;
+        if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+          LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] inline-3D canvas resolve dropped "
+                     << dropped << " of "
+                     << frame.inline_3d_canvas_resources.size()
+                     << " layers (no mailbox); left UNSUPPRESSED so the page "
+                        "draws them (drops="
+                     << (n + 1) << ")";
+        }
+      }
+    }
+    if (skia_output_surface_) {
       // Phase-1 identity: the GPU stage keys its per-target weave state by the
       // element token, so carry {rect, id} rather than bare rects.
       std::vector<SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dRect> weave_rects;
@@ -1013,8 +1060,8 @@ bool Display::DrawAndSwap(const DrawAndSwapParams& params) {
       for (const auto& r : frame.inline_3d_rects) {
         weave_rects.push_back({r.rect, r.id});
       }
-      skia_output_surface_->SetInline3DRects(
-          std::move(weave_rects), resolve(frame.inline_3d_canvas_resources));
+      skia_output_surface_->SetInline3DRects(std::move(weave_rects),
+                                             std::move(resolved_canvas));
     }
     // DisplayXR inline-3D (browser#22 B): hand the renderer the resources the
     // weave draw-back is the single source for, so their quads are not painted
@@ -1033,9 +1080,12 @@ bool Display::DrawAndSwap(const DrawAndSwapParams& params) {
         // excludes already-suppressed resources from its over set); the
         // difference clip punches its over-tile part out of the page raster and
         // the over-plane composite draws it back on top.
-        for (const auto& layer : frame.inline_3d_canvas_resources) {
-          suppressed.insert(layer.resource_id);
-        }
+        //
+        // (web#12) RESOLVED ids only — see the block above. This set is now, by
+        // construction, exactly the set of canvas layers the GPU stage received
+        // in |inline_3d_canvas_layers_|, which is what lets that stage reason
+        // about whether |output| at a tile rect is a hole or real page pixels.
+        suppressed = std::move(resolved_canvas_ids);
       }
       renderer_->SetInline3dSuppressedResources(std::move(suppressed));
     }
diff --git a/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc b/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc
index 93ed2a62b8d58..7188ce68a2978 100644
--- a/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc
+++ b/components/viz/service/display_embedder/skia_output_surface_impl_on_gpu.cc
@@ -2820,9 +2820,30 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::MaybeWeaveRootRenderPass(SkSurface* surface,
   // inline_3d_rects_ are window-relative device-px, so only the window-sized root
   // render pass (== the whole composited page, the CEF pageTex equivalent under
   // DComp) carries them. Non-root overlay passes are element-sized — skip them.
+  //
+  // (web#12) This bail was SILENT, and it is one of the ranked candidates
+  // for a multi-second window in which NO weave submit is issued at all —
+  // during which every tile is a suppressed hole with no submit to repair it
+  // and nothing in the log to say why. It cannot simply be logged: it fires
+  // routinely and harmlessly for element-sized (non-root) overlay passes, so a
+  // per-event line would be noise on every healthy run. What is diagnostic is a
+  // RUN of them with no root-pass weave in between — that, and only that, is a
+  // stall. So count consecutive bails, reset on the first pass that reaches the
+  // weave, and speak up once the run passes ~1 s at 60 fps, then on the 0070
+  // 1-in-300 cadence for as long as it lasts.
+  static int s_consecutive_bails = 0;
   if (gfx::Size(surface->width(), surface->height()) != size_) {
+    const int n = s_consecutive_bails++;
+    if (n == 60 || (n > 60 && (n % 300) == 0)) {
+      LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] MaybeWeaveRootRenderPass: " << (n + 1)
+                 << " consecutive passes with no root-sized pass (last "
+                 << surface->width() << "x" << surface->height()
+                 << " vs surface " << size_.ToString()
+                 << ") — inline-3D has not woven for this whole run";
+    }
     return;
   }
+  s_consecutive_bails = 0;
   static bool logged = false;
   if (!logged) {
     logged = true;
@@ -3020,12 +3041,19 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
           return c;
         }
       }
+      // (web#12) Promoted from VLOG(1) to the 0070 throttle discipline. This
+      // line separates "the matcher missed" from "the resource is gone", which
+      // is precisely the distinction the no-clean-resource gate below acts on —
+      // and at VLOG(1) it was invisible in every default-verbosity run, so
+      // "no id-miss lines" was never evidence of absence.
       static int id_miss_dbg = 0;
-      if ((id_miss_dbg++ % 60) == 0) {
-        VLOG(1) << "[DisplayXR] GPU canvas-layer id miss: id=" << id.ToString()
-                << " rect=" << r.ToString() << " among "
-                << inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size()
-                << " canvas layers — falling back to overlap match";
+      const int n = id_miss_dbg++;
+      if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+        LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] GPU canvas-layer id miss: id="
+                   << id.ToString() << " rect=" << r.ToString() << " among "
+                   << inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size()
+                   << " canvas layers — falling back to overlap match (misses="
+                   << (n + 1) << ")";
       }
     }
     for (const auto& c : inline_3d_canvas_layers_) {
@@ -3037,9 +3065,48 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
         return c;
       }
     }
+    // (web#12) The null return was logged NOWHERE — the single most
+    // consequential silence in this path, because it is what routed the tile
+    // into the output-snapshot fallback and therefore into weaving the
+    // suppression hole. Count it at the lookup site, with the context (id,
+    // rect, list size) that says WHICH of the two causes it was: an empty list
+    // means display.cc resolved nothing this frame (so nothing is suppressed
+    // and the composited output is real page pixels); a non-empty one means the
+    // matcher missed a layer that IS suppressed.
+    {
+      static int null_dbg = 0;
+      const int n = null_dbg++;
+      if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+        LOG(ERROR)
+            << "[DisplayXR] GPU canvas-layer lookup returned NO resource:"
+            << " id=" << id.ToString() << " rect=" << r.ToString()
+            << " layers=" << inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size()
+            << " (misses=" << (n + 1) << ")";
+      }
+    }
     return SkiaOutputSurface::Inline3dLayerMailbox();
   };
 
+  // (web#12) Which canvas layers actually reached the weave this frame, keyed
+  // by mailbox. Filled by attempt_submit on success; read by the per-tile
+  // recovery draw below, which repaints exactly the on-screen canvas layers
+  // that are NOT in here — the tiles display.cc suppressed and the weave then
+  // declined.
+  std::vector<gpu::Mailbox> submitted_canvas_mailboxes;
+  submitted_canvas_mailboxes.reserve(inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size());
+  // Plain loop rather than std::find: <algorithm> is only pulled in on the
+  // Apple branch of this file's includes, and a handful of mailboxes is not
+  // worth an include change in a header-fragile TU.
+  [[maybe_unused]] const auto canvas_was_submitted =
+      [&](const gpu::Mailbox& m) {
+        for (const gpu::Mailbox& s : submitted_canvas_mailboxes) {
+          if (s == m) {
+            return true;
+          }
+        }
+        return false;
+      };
+
   // |key| identifies the target in |displayxr_targets_|; |t| indexes this
   // frame's rects. Everything the submit touches now hangs off the keyed state,
   // so a target's provider slot / scratch image / in-flight rect stay with it
@@ -3066,6 +3133,44 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
     const gfx::Rect clean_sbs_rect = clean_sbs.rect;  // UNCLIPPED full tile
     const gfx::RectF clean_sbs_uv = clean_sbs.uv;     // resource sub-rect (#73)
 
+    // ---- (web#12) NEVER WEAVE THE HOLE ----
+    //
+    // |inline_3d_canvas_layers_| is now EXACTLY the set display.cc suppressed
+    // from the page raster this frame (that is what "resolve before suppress"
+    // bought us). So it answers, on its own, the only question that matters
+    // here: is |output| at |rect| real page pixels, or a hole?
+    //
+    //   * list EMPTY  -> nothing was suppressed, |output| still holds the
+    //   page's
+    //                    own side-by-side canvas quad. Snapshotting it is the
+    //                    pre-browser#18 weave source and is still correct; let
+    //                    it through.
+    //   * list NON-EMPTY, no match -> canvas quads WERE removed from |output|,
+    //                    and this tile has no clean resource to weave from.
+    //                    Snapshotting would weave the punched hole and report
+    //                    SUCCESS — the entire mechanism of this bug. Skip the
+    //                    tile's submit entry outright; the per-tile recovery
+    //                    draw below repaints it from the resource it does hold,
+    //                    and if it holds none the tile was never suppressed in
+    //                    the first place and the page already drew it.
+    //
+    // Skipping is not a degradation of a working path: the "working" path here
+    // produced a black tile. Returning false is also already wired correctly
+    // downstream — the caller clears |pending_rect| / |drawn_rect| for this
+    // target, so phase (B) will not draw a stale region back for it either.
+    if (clean_sbs_mailbox.IsZero() && !inline_3d_canvas_layers_.empty()) {
+      static int s_noclean = 0;
+      const int n = s_noclean++;
+      if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+        LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] weave tile skipped: no clean canvas resource"
+                   << " (id=" << inline_3d_rects_[t].id.ToString()
+                   << " rect=" << rect.ToString()
+                   << " layers=" << inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size()
+                   << " skips=" << (n + 1) << ")";
+      }
+      return false;
+    }
+
     // B4d GPU-resident weave (DComp path, prefer_zero_copy). Keep the
     // composited sub-rect on the GPU instead of the readPixels roundtrip:
     // Skia-copy it into an owned scratch D3DImageBacking, ProduceOverlay its
@@ -3190,6 +3295,18 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
               }
             }
           }
+          // (web#12) The tile HAS a resolved canvas resource — so it IS one of
+          // the quads display.cc removed from the page raster — and we failed
+          // to open it (ProduceSkia / BeginScopedReadAccess / CreateSkImage).
+          // The output snapshot below is therefore guaranteed to be the hole,
+          // and weaving it would report success while presenting black. Refuse:
+          // fail() drops to the CPU-readback fallback, whose own web#12 gate
+          // (it reads the same |output|) then declines the tile properly.
+          if (!src_img && !clean_sbs_mailbox.IsZero()) {
+            return fail(
+                "clean canvas resource unusable — refusing to weave the "
+                "suppression hole");
+          }
           if (!src_img) {
             src_img = output->makeImageSnapshot(
                 SkIRect::MakeXYWH(rect.x(), rect.y(), rw, rh));
@@ -3407,6 +3524,9 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
         // Remember where this target was submitted so phase (B) draws the woven
         // pixels at the rect the runtime placed them, not a later drifted rect.
         st.pending_rect = rect;
+        if (!clean_sbs_mailbox.IsZero()) {
+          submitted_canvas_mailboxes.push_back(clean_sbs_mailbox);
+        }
         return true;
       }
     }
@@ -3416,6 +3536,32 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
     // input. Ganesh has a synchronous SkSurface::readPixels; Graphite has no sync
     // readback, so it uses the blocking-async path. This reads the clean pre-
     // weave page (phase (A) runs before the woven draw-back in (B)).
+    //
+    // ---- (web#12) …and "the clean pre-weave page" is exactly what it stopped
+    // being when browser#22 landed suppression. This path sources ONLY from
+    // |output| — it never looks at the clean canvas resource — so on any frame
+    // where canvas quads were removed from the page raster it reads the hole
+    // and weaves it, with no gate of any kind. It was the second, unguarded
+    // mouth of the same mechanism.
+    //
+    // The gate is the same one-line question as above: did suppression fire
+    // this frame? If it did, |output| at |rect| is not a source. Fall out and
+    // let the per-tile recovery draw put the tile back from its own resource.
+    // If it did not (an empty layer list — the legacy no-canvas-resource
+    // channel, and the GL output-device path), this readback is still the
+    // correct weave source and is untouched.
+    if (!inline_3d_canvas_layers_.empty()) {
+      static int s_cpu_hole = 0;
+      const int n = s_cpu_hole++;
+      if (n < 3 || (n % 300) == 0) {
+        LOG(ERROR) << "[DisplayXR] weave tile skipped: CPU readback would "
+                      "sample the suppression hole (rect="
+                   << rect.ToString()
+                   << " layers=" << inline_3d_canvas_layers_.size()
+                   << " skips=" << (n + 1) << ")";
+      }
+      return false;
+    }
     const SkImageInfo info = SkImageInfo::Make(
         rw, rh, kBGRA_8888_SkColorType, kPremul_SkAlphaType,
         SkColorSpace::MakeSRGB());
@@ -3581,49 +3727,60 @@ void SkiaOutputSurfaceImplOnGpu::WeaveCompositedSurface(SkSurface* output,
   }
 
 #if BUILDFLAG(IS_WIN)
-  // (browser#87) Liveness gate: skip the Phase (B) draw-back entirely when this
-  // frame wove nothing, rather than blitting a woven texture that still holds
-  // the previous page's pixels. Batch mode only: the batch weave completes
-  // synchronously inside SubmitBatchSync, so "nothing submitted" there really
-  // means "nothing woven". Async mode deliberately keeps the last woven output
-  // visible while a submit is in flight — that is its one-frame-lag contract.
-  if (batch && !wove_this_frame) {
